course: everyday
lesson: 421
title: Screen-time rules and arguments
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every night at eight o'clock Kai's house turned into a courtroom. «Five more minutes!» «You said that twenty minutes ago.» «That was a different five minutes!» Kai's dad finally sat him down on a Sunday, when nobody was in the middle of a game, and they wrote the rules together: one hour on school days, more at weekends, and a ten-minute warning before the end. The arguments did not vanish, but they shrank from a nightly war to the odd grumble, and Kai admitted that the warning helped most, because he could finish a match instead of being yanked out of it.
explain: Leo tried the classic move: «But Theo gets three hours!» His mum said: «Then go and live at Theo's.» The next day Leo found out that Theo's console switched itself off after one hour, set by his parents, and that the «three hours» had happened once, on a snow day. Leo never used the Theo argument again. Jayden's family has a simpler picture: less on school days, more at weekends, because sleep and homework matter most on school nights.
explain: Ollie thought he was clever. He hid his tablet under the bathroom towels and played at night. After a week of grumpy mornings, a falling-asleep-in-maths moment and a dead battery that gave him away, his parents found out. The worst part was not losing the tablet for a week; it was that they stopped believing his «I'm not playing». Getting trust back took much longer than the punishment. Ruby lost her screen time for a different reason: she called her mum «a total pain» when told to stop. She got it back after a proper sorry.
explain: Some kids found better ways. Priya earns extra minutes by doing chores, and her bedroom has never been so tidy. Zara wrote a list of reasons for more screen time: «I finish homework first, I play with my cousin in Australia, I have not been rude once this month.» Her parents gave her thirty extra minutes at weekends, because the list was calm and true. In Noah's family the no-screens dinner rule includes Mum's phone, which makes it feel fair, and once a month Mia's parents ask: «Are the rules still working?» and change what does not.
fact: Screen-time rules are usually made to protect sleep, homework, health and family time, not to spoil fun.
fact: Rules work better when a family makes them together, at a calm time, and not in the middle of an argument.
fact: A warning a few minutes before the end makes it easier to stop, because you can finish a match or save.
fact: Another family's rules are not a reason for yours to change, and stories about «three hours» are often exaggerated.
fact: Many consoles and tablets have parental settings that switch play off after a set time.
fact: Hiding a device to play in secret usually shows in tiredness and grumpy mornings, and it breaks trust.
fact: Trust is lost quickly and earned back slowly, often more slowly than any punishment lasts, because people need to see you be honest many times.
fact: A calm, honest request with good reasons is more likely to win more time than shouting or sulking.
fact: Rules feel fairer when grown-ups follow them too, for example no phones at dinner for anyone.
fact: Good families check their rules now and then and change the ones that no longer work, because children grow and need different rules.
exercise: Why did Kai's dad choose a Sunday afternoon to write the rules? | Nobody was in the middle of a game or angry | Sunday is the only legal day to make family rules | Kai was not allowed to play on Sundays | The shops were closed so nobody could buy games
exercise: Which part of the new rules helped Kai most? | The ten-minute warning before the end | The longer time to play at the weekend | That his dad played with him | That the rules were written in pen
exercise: How did Kai probably feel when he was pulled out of a match with no warning? | Cheated and angry, because he lost the match | Pleased, because he was getting tired of it anyway | Calm, because the game saves by itself | Proud, because he obeyed at once
exercise: What did Leo find out about Theo's «three hours»? | It had happened once, on a snow day | Theo plays three hours every single night | Theo's parents never set any limit | Theo plays five hours, not three
exercise: Why does «but Theo gets three hours» rarely work? | Each family makes its own rules for its own reasons | Parents never listen to anything children say | Theo's parents are stricter than Leo's | It only works if Theo says it himself
exercise: Why does Jayden get less screen time on school days? | Sleep and homework matter most on school nights | Games cost more to play on school days, in the evenings | Consoles work worse during the week | His friends only play at weekends
exercise: How did Ollie's parents find out he was playing at night? | He was grumpy, sleepy and the battery was dead | His sister found his secret and read it aloud at tea | The tablet sent them a message at night | His teacher phoned to say he had a game
exercise: What was the worst thing Ollie lost? | His parents' trust in his word | His tablet for one whole week, maybe two | His best ever score in the game | His place in the top maths group
exercise: Why does trust take longer to win back than a punishment lasts? | People need to see you be honest many times | Parents enjoy keeping punishments going for as long as they can | Trust can only come back on birthdays | The tablet has to be reset first
exercise: Why did Ruby lose her screen time? | She was rude to her mum when told to stop | She played a game that was rated for adults | She forgot to charge her tablet | She played during dinner with guests
exercise: What did Ruby do that was wise afterwards? | She said a proper sorry to her mum | She hid her tablet in the bathroom like Ollie did | She sulked until her mum gave in | She told her mum Theo gets three hours
exercise: Why did Zara's list work better than shouting? | It was calm, true and showed she was responsible | It was very long and had many pages | Her parents could not read her writing | She said Theo gets more than she does
exercise: How does Priya earn extra minutes? | By doing chores around the house | By playing only quiet, calm games | By promising to stop complaining about it | By paying for it with her pocket money
exercise: Why does Noah's family include Mum's phone in the no-screens dinner rule? | It feels fair when grown-ups follow it too | Mum's phone is too loud at dinner | Mum is the one who plays most | The phone company asked them to
exercise: Why do Mia's parents check the rules every month? | Children grow and rules may need to change | They forget the rules every month | The law says family rules must change every month | Mia gets a new game every month
exercise: Which kind of courage did Ollie need after being caught? | Owning up and keeping his word for weeks | Hiding the tablet in a better place | Arguing that everyone in his class plays at night | Blaming his sister for telling on him
test: «MUM just 5 more mins!!» «you said that 20 min ago». What would most likely stop this nightly row? | Rules made together, with a warning before the end | Taking the console apart every evening | Letting Kai play as long as he likes | Shouting louder until one person gives up
test: Leo: «Theo gets 3 hrs, so I should too». Is that a good argument? | No, each family has its own rules and reasons | Yes, parents have to copy what other parents allow | Yes, if Theo is older than Leo | No, because Theo is not a real friend
test: Ollie yawning in maths, teacher: «late night again?» What most likely caused it? | Playing on a hidden tablet at night | Eating too much breakfast that day | Doing too much homework the night before | Sitting too close to the classroom window
test: «just hide ur tablet in the bathroom lol they'll never know». What usually happens? | Tiredness gives it away and trust is broken | Parents never notice anything at all | You get more sleep because you feel calm and relaxed | The tablet charges itself in the towels
test: Ruby: «ugh you're such a pain, mum». Mum: «Screen time's gone.» What would help Ruby most now? | A real sorry and calmer words next time | Slamming the door to show how upset she is | Saying her friend's mum is nicer | Waiting in silence for a whole month
test: Zara wants more time. Which plan has the best chance? | A calm list of true reasons at a quiet moment | Asking twenty times in a row at bedtime | Crying loudly until her parents give in | Telling them everyone else gets five hours
test: Priya: «hoovered the whole house, that's 30 mins innit». What kind of rule does her family have? | Earning screen time by doing chores | Losing screen time for being too noisy | Screen time only on her birthday and at Christmas | No screens at all for anyone, ever, in the house
test: Noah's mum checks her phone at dinner. Noah: «oi, no screens, that's the rule». Who is right? | Noah, the rule is for everyone, Mum too | Mum, rules are only for children | Nobody, phones are not screens | Mum, if the message is from work or the school
test: Theo's console turns off after an hour by itself. Who set that? | His parents, with the parental settings | The game company, for every child in the country | Theo himself, to save power and the battery | The console decides for itself by his age
test: Kai: «the warning is pointless, 10 mins is nothing». What is the warning really for? | Finishing a match or saving before stopping | Giving extra time to play just one more game | Telling the neighbours the game is over | Letting the console cool down safely
test: Mia's dad: «Are the rules still working for you?» Why is this a good question? | Rules may need to change as kids grow | It is a trick to take more of her time away | He has forgotten what the family rules are | He wants Mia to make all the rules herself
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which families customarily institute limitations upon the duration of recreational screen use. | The protection of sleep, study, health and family time | The reduction of expenditure upon electricity | The prevention of damage to the devices concerned | The compliance with a national statutory limit
exam: It is given that a pupil concealed a device in order to play during the night. Select the consequence most consistent with the established facts. | The diminution of parental trust, slowly restored | The permanent improvement of his concentration at school | The immediate award of additional screen time | The absence of any consequence whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an appeal to the rules of another household? | It is unpersuasive, each family establishing its own rules | It obliges the parents to adopt the identical rules | It is persuasive whenever the other child is older | It constitutes the most effective form of argument
exam: Determine which approach is most likely to secure an extension of permitted screen time. | A calm, truthful request supported by sound reasons | A repeated demand made at the hour of bedtime | A refusal to speak until the request is granted | A claim that all classmates receive unlimited time
